  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A slow moving cold front approaches and moves\nthrough the area on Friday, bringing the potential for heavy \nrain to portions of the area beginning on Thursday.\n\nUpper level troughing pivoting over the Great Lakes region\nslowly tracks eastward through Friday, before the upper low\ntracks overhead on Saturday. At the surface, this translates a\nslow moving cold front approaching the region on Thursday,\nbefore slowly moving overhead and stalling nearby through\nFriday.\n\nAs the front approaches from the west, precipitation chances\nincrease for those in the western portions of the forecast area\nthroughout the day on Thursday. Those along and east of the\nmetros will stay primarily dry with 30% chance of precipitation\nor less. Precipitation chances increase Thursday night into\nFriday as the cold front approaches and moisture increases.\nBeneficial rainfall is expected with REFS ensembles showing a 24\nhour precipitation total swath stretching from southern \nDelaware across the New Jersey coast of 1 to 2 inches with\nlocally higher amounts up to 2 to 3 inches possible. Further \nwest, precipitation totals range from a trace to an inch. \nExactly where the heaviest precipitation totals line up remains \nuncertain and will be dependent on where the front stalls. \n\nThe cold front departs offshore on Saturday, with mostly dry \nconditions returning to the forecast. A spotty shower cannot be\nfully ruled out over the weekend with the upper low lingering\nacross the Northeast. High temperatures will be noticeably \ncooler with highs in the upper 50s to mid 60s. Overnight low \ntemperatures will be in the 40s for most. Temperatures continue \ncooling Sunday into early next week with highs in the upper 50s \nto low 60s and overnight lows in the 30s for most (40s metro \nareas).",
